## Servers

If the OpenAPI spec defines multiple servers, the generated package exposes each as a `*ServerConfiguration` variable (e.g., `Server0`, `Server1`, ...) plus an `AllServers` slice. Pass the desired server's URL to the client:

```go
url, err := {{packageName}}.Server0.URL(nil)
if err != nil {
	log.Fatal(err)
}

client, err := {{packageName}}.New{{clientClassName}}WithToken(url, "your-token", nil)
if err != nil {
	log.Fatal(err)
}
```

{{/hasServers}}

## Testing

The `Authenticator` interface is the seam for tests: substitute a fake authenticator that returns a known header map, and assert your code calls the API the way you expect.

```go
type fakeAuth struct{}

func (fakeAuth) Host() string { return "https://api.example.com" }
func (fakeAuth) AuthHeaders() map[string]string {
    return map[string]string{"Authorization": "Bearer test-token"}
}
func (fakeAuth) QueryParams() map[string]string  { return nil }
func (fakeAuth) CookieParams() map[string]string { return nil }

client := {{packageName}}.New{{clientClassName}}(fakeAuth{}, nil)
```

## Error Handling

Every failure the SDK produces satisfies the `{{errorPrefix}}Error` interface, so one `errors.As` against it catches all of them. It has four direct kinds: `ApiError` (an HTTP response or a transport failure), `SerializationError` (a request or response body that cannot be encoded or decoded), `OAuth2ServerError` (the token endpoint answered with an error) and `OAuth2TokenError` (the token response was unusable). The last two surface from the first authenticated call, so they are siblings of `ApiError`, not subtypes of it: an `ApiError` branch alone lets them escape. The error hierarchy is:

- `{{errorPrefix}}Error` (root interface)
  - `SerializationError`
  - `OAuth2ServerError`
  - `OAuth2TokenError`
  - `ApiError`
    - `ClientError` (4xx)
      - `BadRequestError` (400)
      - `UnauthorizedError` (401)
      - `ForbiddenError` (403)
      - `NotFoundError` (404)
      - `ConflictError` (409)
      - `UnprocessableEntityError` (422)
      - `TooManyRequestsError` (429)
    - `ServerError` (5xx)
      - `InternalServerError` (500)
    - `NetworkError` (no HTTP response, status 0)
      - `NetworkTimeoutError` (the request timed out, status 0)

```go
import (
    "context"
    "errors"
    "fmt"

    "{{packageName}}/pkg"
)

{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{#operation}}{{#-first}}{{#returnType}}_, {{/returnType}}{{/-first}}{{/operation}}err := client.{{baseName}}.{{#operation}}{{#-first}}{{operationId}}(context.Background(){{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}, {{#isPath}}{{paramName}}{{/isPath}}{{#isBody}}{{paramName}}{{/isBody}}{{#isOptions}}nil{{/isOptions}}{{#isServer}}nil{{/isServer}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}){{/-first}}{{/operation}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}
if err != nil {
    var notFound *{{packageName}}.NotFoundError
    var clientErr *{{packageName}}.ClientError
    var serverErr *{{packageName}}.ServerError
    var apiErr *{{packageName}}.ApiError
    var root {{packageName}}.{{errorPrefix}}Error
    if errors.As(err, &notFound) {
        fmt.Printf("Not found: %s\n", notFound.Error())
    } else if errors.As(err, &clientErr) {
        fmt.Printf("Client error %d: %s\n", clientErr.StatusCode(), clientErr.Error())
    } else if errors.As(err, &serverErr) {
        fmt.Printf("Server error: %s\n", serverErr.Error())
    } else if errors.As(err, &apiErr) {
        fmt.Printf("API error %d: %s\n", apiErr.StatusCode(), apiErr.Error())
    } else if errors.As(err, &root) {
        fmt.Printf("SDK error: %s\n", root.Error())
    }
}
```

## Configuration

### Custom Transport Options

```go
timeout := 5000
transport, err := {{packageName}}.NewTransportOptionsBuilder().
    Proxy("http://proxy:3128").
    Timeout(&timeout).
    Build()
if err != nil {
    return err
}

client := {{packageName}}.New{{clientClassName}}(authenticator, transport)
```

### Retries

A request is retried up to `MaxRetries` times (default 2; `MaxRetries(0)` disables retries) after a network error that happened before a response and after a 408, 409, 429 or 5xx (not 501) response. The wait starts at 0.5 seconds, doubles up to 8 seconds, loses up to 25% to jitter and is replaced by a `Retry-After` header (seconds or an HTTP date, at most 60 seconds). The retries never extend the end-to-end `Timeout`, a cancelled context stops them, and every retry rebuilds the headers, authentication included. A `multipart/form-data` request is sent once and never retried, because its file parts are streams.

```go
transport, err := {{packageName}}.NewTransportOptionsBuilder().
    MaxRetries(0).
    Build()
```

A 401 on an OAuth2 or OpenID Connect request calls `InvalidateAccessToken` on the authenticator (the refresh token is kept) and replays the request once; a second 401 is an `UnauthorizedError`.

### Custom HTTP client

```go
httpClient := &http.Client{Transport: &http.Transport{}}
transport, err := {{packageName}}.NewTransportOptionsBuilder().
    HttpClient(httpClient).
    Build()
```

The SDK sends every request through your client (anything with a `Do(*http.Request)` method; `RoundTripper(rt)` wraps an `http.RoundTripper`) and never closes it. Combining it with `Proxy`, `CACertPath` or `VerifySsl(false)` fails at `Build()` with `ErrTransportConflict`.

### Cancellation

Every operation takes a `ctx context.Context` as its first parameter; cancelling it aborts the request and the error satisfies `errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)`. It is never reported as `NetworkTimeoutError`.

```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
go func() { time.Sleep(time.Second); cancel() }()
{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{#operation}}{{#-first}}{{#returnType}}_, {{/returnType}}{{/-first}}{{/operation}}err := client.{{baseName}}.{{#operation}}{{#-first}}{{operationId}}(ctx{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}, {{#isPath}}{{paramName}}{{/isPath}}{{#isBody}}{{paramName}}{{/isBody}}{{#isOptions}}nil{{/isOptions}}{{#isServer}}nil{{/isServer}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}){{/-first}}{{/operation}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}
if errors.Is(err, context.Canceled) {
    fmt.Println("cancelled")
}
```

### Raw responses, timeouts and the request id

Each operation has a `...WithHTTPInfo` variant returning `*ApiResult[T]` (`StatusCode`, `Data`, `RawBody`, `Headers`). `Timeout` is one end-to-end budget in positive milliseconds. An `X-Request-ID` header is added to each request unless you set one.

### Credentials

`New{{clientClassName}}WithToken(host, accessToken, transportOptions)` resolves each credential once, when the client is built: the argument wins, then `{{envVarPrefix}}_BASE_URL` for the host and `{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN` for the token; an empty value counts as unset, and a credential that cannot be resolved returns `ErrMissingCredential`. `New{{clientClassName}}WithTokenProvider(host, provider, transportOptions)` takes a `func(ctx context.Context) (string, error)` that runs for every request.

`New{{clientClassName}}WithCredentials(host, {{packageName}}.Credentials{...}, transportOptions)` takes one optional authenticator per security scheme, named after the scheme; each operation uses the credential of the scheme its spec requires and there is no per-call override. Each security scheme has its own authenticator constructor, which resolves a credential the same way: a value you pass wins, an empty string is read once from the scheme's variable (an empty variable counts as unset), and a credential found in neither place returns an error matching `auth.ErrMissingCredential` that names the variable. A Bearer scheme also has `New<Scheme>AuthenticatorWithProvider(host, provider)`, which reads no variable.
{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

{{#securitySchemeEnvVars}}
- `{{envName}}`: the `{{field}}` of the `{{schemeId}}` scheme
{{/securitySchemeEnvVars}}
{{/hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

### Per-call options

Every operation, also one with no parameters and no security, takes `RequestOption` values as its last arguments: `WithTimeout(time.Duration)`, `WithHeaders(map[string]string)`, `WithMaxRetries(int)` and, for an operation with several request content types, `WithRequestContentType(string)`. An option left out keeps the client's setting.

### Response compression, logging and versioning

Responses in `gzip`, `deflate`, `br` and `zstd` are decoded for you. The SDK writes no log lines: wrap the transport with `TransportOptionsBuilder.RoundTripper` to log requests. The module follows semantic versioning, and Go is supported from the oldest release that has not reached end of life.

## API Methods

Every operation takes a `ctx context.Context` as its first parameter; cancelling it aborts the request (`errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)`), and `client.Close()` is graceful and idempotent: requests in flight finish and new ones return `ErrClientClosed`. Each API group is exposed as a typed field on the client (e.g., `client.{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{baseName}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`). API classes have methods that correspond to OpenAPI operations, accepting typed request parameters and returning typed response models.

## Models

Models are generated as Go structs in the `models` package.

```go
import "{{packageName}}/pkg/models"

{{#models}}{{#model}}{{#-first}}m := models.{{classname}}{}{{/-first}}{{/model}}{{/models}}
```

## Binary / File Uploads

File upload parameters and binary request bodies are typed as `io.Reader`; an `*os.File` satisfies it, and so does `bytes.NewReader` or `strings.NewReader`. Binary response bodies are returned as `[]byte`.

## Comment Style

Never place a comment on the same line as code. Use block comments (`/* ... */`).

```good
/* This explains the logic */
x := 1
```

```bad
// This explains the logic
x := 1
```
